    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d307">

                <pb facs="0533" n="419" sameAs="frus:frus1931v01/pg419"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15a3/1583: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Italy</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Garrett</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Rome</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-05-26T18:00:00+01:00">May 26, 1931—6
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received May 26—3:05 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>81. Grandi returned yesterday from Geneva. I have not seen him, but I have
                    ascertained from a Foreign Office official who came back with him that it is
                    believed that the tension between Italy and France has diminished owing to
                    opposition on Italy’s part to the Austro-German Customs Union, which came as a
                    welcome surprise to France; also that the French attitude toward Italy is so
                    changed that it now <pb facs="0534" n="420" xml:id="pg420"/> appears likely that
                    after all something may come out of the Franco-Italian negotiations. Paris will
                    receive cipher text by mail.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Garrett</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
